WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.760
Last April, Anthropic launched Project
Glasswing, unleashing a withheld frontier

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.600
model called Claude Mythos Preview to hunt
for software bugs

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.080
sweeping across critical infrastructure.

4
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.440
In a single month of automated scanning,
the AI found

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.960
over 10,000 high or critical severity
vulnerabilities inside critical digital

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.560
systems. The project is a consortium of 50
major tech

7
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.040
partners, including Google, Apple,
Microsoft, and Cloudflare, who used it

8
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.160
to find vulnerabilities before adversaries
could.

9
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.520
With industry giants involved and a
massive tally of verified

10
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.920
bugs successfully caught, automated
discovery looks like a monumental win

11
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.360
for AI safety.

12
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.360
This pie chart represents the 10,000
vulnerabilities discovered.

13
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.560
That sliver at the top represents the bugs
patched, under

14
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.120
1%. Zooming in on the 530 disclosed bugs,
the patch

15
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.200
rate is 14.1%.

16
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.840
The vast majority remain unpatched.

17
00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.840
The people responsible for fixing these
vulnerabilities

18
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.440
have explicitly asked Anthropic to slow
down its disclosures.

19
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.640
Open source maintainers are working on
their own time, usually

20
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.720
without dedicated security teams.

21
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.920
They need weeks to design and verify a
secure patch

22
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.360
before the next wave of AI-generated bug
reports arrives.

23
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:20.440
Project Glasswing accelerated the
discovery side of the equation without

24
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.920
providing any solution for the fixing
side.

25
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.760
The result is a verified, expanding
inventory of software flaws

26
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.320
that no one has the capacity to resolve.

27
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.720
Historically, software security progressed
slowly because human analysts had to

28
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.200
manually hunt for complex vulnerabilities.

29
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.560
The friction of discovery kept the volume
of bugs manageable.

30
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.480
This pipeline shows the shift.

31
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.920
Discovery scales with compute, meaning
finding a flaw is just

32
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.720
processing power, flooding the system's
front.

33
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.320
But it hits a hard limit.

34
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.400
Verifying, coordinating, and writing the
patch still relies on finite

35
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.240
human capacity. While defenders are
trapped in that bottleneck, attackers

36
00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.760
are moving faster.

37
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.000
The security industry measures this
through the weaponization window, the

38
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.240
time between a vulnerability being
disclosed and an attacker successfully

39
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.680
exploiting it. This chart maps the
mismatch.

40
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.040
The flat line shows human patching speed
holding steady at

41
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.560
a two-week average.

42
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.680
The red curve shows attacker weaponization
dropping from 771 days

43
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.600
to single-digit hours.

44
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.360
AI agents can generate exploits in under
five minutes.

45
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.200
A two-week defensive cycle cannot compete
with a five-minute offensive

46
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.040
strike. The defense is failing due to a
structural mismatch

47
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.200
between machine-speed exploitation and
human-paced remediation.

48
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.640
It helps to clarify exactly what Claude
Mytho's preview is

49
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.080
capable of right now.

50
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.400
The model is highly proficient at static,
tactical environments, routinely

51
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.840
beating capture-the-flag security puzzles.

52
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.800
What it struggles with is the broader
strategic layer.

53
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.280
Independent enterprise testing shows
current models fail to maintain operational

54
00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.960
state when trying to autonomously breach a
live, actively defended

55
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.600
corporate network. High scores on static
benchmarks often overstate real-world

56
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.560
capability. Exposed to live, unreleased
tests without internet access, model

57
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.120
performance drops by half as they lose the
ability to

58
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.360
retrieve data from published write-ups.

59
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.440
AI cannot autonomously run a complete
strategic network siege yet,

60
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.720
but by automating the tactical steps, it
easily hands human

61
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.760
attackers flawlessly executed exploits on
demand.

62
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.360
Anthropic and its consortium currently
keep this capability locked behind

63
00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.000
their own servers, but the industry tracks
a metric called

64
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.200
the diffusion window, the shrinking amount
of time defenders have

65
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.520
before this capability leaks to the
public.

66
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.600
Looking at this timeline, independent
benchmarks quantify that exact buffer.

67
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.520
Defenders have between 5.7 and 13.1 months
before the capability

68
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.440
currently restricted to the closed source
frontier becomes decentralized.

69
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.760
Once that window closes, Mythos-level
exploit generation becomes available in

70
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.440
OpenWait AI models.

71
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.880
Anyone can download them, host them
offline, and bypass API

72
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.440
rate limits or content safety policies.

73
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.960
The technical skill barrier required to
weaponize these bugs effectively

74
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.280
disappears. Complex exploitation shifts
from a specialized craft to a

75
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.800
commodity accessible to anyone with a
high-end local GPU.

76
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.240
When that barrier falls, the 10,000
unpatched bugs sitting in

77
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.200
the Glasswing backlog and the hundreds
more actively waiting for

78
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.560
a patch will still be sitting there,
perfectly exposed.

79
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.520
The security industry has a narrow window
to pivot.

80
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.600
We must deploy automated patch generation
directly alongside Discovery and

81
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.360
inject structured financial capacity into
the open source infrastructure currently

82
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.800
drowning under the workload.

83
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.600
Finding 10,000 bugs in a month shows that
the bottleneck

84
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.440
is no longer analytical.

85
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.680
But if AI only accelerates how we identify
targets without

86
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.280
automating the fix, we are simply doing
reconnaissance for our

87
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:51.720
adversaries.

